;; talk/institution--structure.scm — structure lens on surfaces/institution.scm.

(objection 'o-institution-structure-1 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-institution-unasked :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/utils.ts" "The leaning is voted-rules, whose text says only automatic behaviour the board voted on happens. The :because then keeps claiming incoming payments and signing add-only settlements automatic with no vote, and two defaults (institution-default-claim-incoming, institution-default-add-only-settlements) implement that. So a fourth option, voted rules plus two unvoted carve-outs, was chosen inside the reasoning and in defaults, and never offered or argued as an option. The carve-out rests on 'carry no loss', but the protocol says receiving beyond the counterparty's collateral share means trusting them: a claimed payment drawn on credit the organisation granted raises its unsecured exposure. The default is labelled risk-on nobody. The cost: the board that was promised nothing happens without its vote finds its exposure growing with no vote, which is the surprise the person's own cannot-list rules out."))

(objection 'o-institution-structure-2 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'institution-default-response-time :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "docs/core/00_QA.md" "A weekend-long response time is set as a default on every new account, risk on the counterparty, and it is decided inside d-institution-reach as a side effect with no option of its own. The protocol sums both sides' response times into the challenge window of a leaving (25 hours by default, the document says the rule may change), so the organisation's own long response time lengthens its own wait every time it leaves a silent counterparty. d-institution-silent leans to ready-to-leave on the argument that each hour is added to that wait. One node adds roughly two days to the wait that another spends its argument removing hours from, and the risk label hides that the holder pays. The number itself, how long the board can be unreachable, is a real choice that has no decision."))

(objection 'o-institution-structure-3 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-institution-setup :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The leaning is a one-time board vote on a setup sheet before the first account, and six defaults say 'until the board votes' on it. Nothing in the surface offers it. No job, screen or flow here signs open-account or the standing settings (the only flows that touch accounts sign pay, credit, savings and leaving), the setup is not shown on any screen, and no screen lets the board approve a settlement that takes from us, though institution-default-add-only-settlements says those wait for the board. The kernel has an open flow (k-open-flow) that this surface neither uses nor offers. So the decision that carries the most risk-bearing defaults has no place where a person makes it, and the defaults would stay in force forever. The cost: the safe defaults are never replaced, or the person finds the setup somewhere the surface does not describe. The setup sheet is also a configuration path beside the runtime's own account opening, which the decision's own changes-if concedes."))

(objection 'o-institution-structure-4 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-institution-signer-change :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The job is to take signing rights from someone who left. The leaning answers a different case: a board that still agrees with one person missing. A departed person who still holds their key keeps their weight and their vote until the organisation moves, so the margin protects against absence and does nothing about a former colleague who can still vote, which the job exists to stop. The leaning also tells the organisation to 'move to a new setup', and that is the heaviest thing it could do: leave every counterparty (a window of 25 hours or more each), reopen every account and re-agree every credit line. No flow, screen or gap describes that move, and the gap names only the missing replacement. The options are also uneven: bring-into-scope is a request to someone else, and company-path is dismissed as out of launch scope by a canon rule, which leaves one option inside the surface's own power."))

(objection 'o-institution-structure-5 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'institution-today :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/htlc/resolve.ts" "The first screen is meant to answer what needs one of us today, and the decision's argument starts with requests waiting for votes and members blocked by their own waiting request. Neither is on the screen: :shows holds five protocol figures, and the board state sits only in a need. Of the five, three are deadlines nobody can act on: a payment in flight ends by itself and the payer cannot recall it before its deadline (the protocol says only the beneficiary can release it, and the payer cancels only after expiry), a fee-notice is a worse fee that will apply whatever the board does, and a dispute window ends when it ends. The screen is organised around the protocol fields that carry a time, not around what a board member can do. The cost: the item that blocks the finance lead's next payment is not on the first screen, and the list is filled with deadlines that need no one. The answer is also two questions joined by 'and'."))

(objection 'o-institution-structure-6 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-institution-silent :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/processing/proposals.ts" "The leaning is that a request to leave is prepared at once when a counterparty is silent, but the protocol has no draft: a prepared request is a proposal, it takes the proposer's one waiting slot and cannot be withdrawn. Whether the wallet puts the proposal by itself (an action nobody was asked about, which d-institution-unasked leans against) or keeps a draft somewhere (state the runtime does not own) is decided silently inside the option. The kernel leaves the silence threshold to each face, and this surface sets none, so 'at once' has no trigger: a threshold too short fills a member's only slot on every lull and blocks that member's payments. Leaving is also three requests with a wait between them (prepare, start, finish) and the decision treats it as one. How many votes it takes, and whether the first two can go in one request, is chosen in the flow's :signs and not as a decision."))
